WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
You submit your React Native app for review, and 24 hours later,

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
an email lands in your inbox citing a missing API declaration.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Apple requires a machine readable record of any sensitive system APIs your app uses.

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.000
The catch for React Native developers is that the framework itself

5
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.000
accesses several of these restricted APIs internally to function.

6
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Your custom JavaScript code might never call these system features directly,

7
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
but because the React Native engine is compiled into your linked binary,

8
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.000
Apple's automated review system detects the access and flags the submission.

9
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.000
If you are using Expo SDK 56 or later on the managed workflow,

10
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.000
the Expo CLI handles this declaration automatically during the pre-built step.

11
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.000
However, if you are running a bear workflow project or adding custom native modules,

12
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.000
you must address this requirement manually.

13
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:52.000
To pass the automated reviewers, you have to create a specific property list file

14
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.000
that translates React Native's internal architecture into a declared checklist.

15
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.000
Now, step one is generating the file.

16
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Open your iOS project in Xcode by selecting the .xc workspace file

17
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.000
rather than the .xc project file to ensure your dependencies link correctly.

18
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.000
In the top menu, go to file, new, then file from template.

19
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Filter your search for privacy and select the app privacy template.

20
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Name the file exactly privacyinfo.xc privacy.

21
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:29.000
In the project navigator, verify that this file sits at the exact same level as your app delegate file.

22
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Simply placing the file in the right directory is not enough.

23
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Excode needs explicit instructions to include this file when it bundles the final application.

24
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Select the new privacy file, open the file inspector on the right-hand panel,

25
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.000
and check the box for target membership assigned to your primary app target.

26
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Leaving that box unchecked causes the compiler to strip the manifest

27
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.000
from your final build archive, resulting in a guaranteed rejection from the app store.

28
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.000
With the file correctly targeted, open it.

29
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.000
Xcode displays a structured editor.

30
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Click the plus button to add a new top level array called privacy access API types.

31
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.000
This diagram maps out the framework's specific API usage.

32
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.000
First, React Native reads and writes to NS user defaults during its bridge initialization

33
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.000
to manage internal framework state.

34
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.000
In the structured editor, add a new row.

35
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:26.000
Select user defaults from the category dropdown and assign it the specific framework level reason code,

36
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.000
CA92.1.

37
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.000
Next, the Metro Bundler run time and the frameworks image caching layers.

38
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Read file timestamps to handle internal cache invalidation.

39
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.000
Add file timestamp APIs as your second category and assign reason code C617.1.

40
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:49.000
Finally, React Native measures elapsed intervals for its performance tracing and animation subsystems

41
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.000
using high precision system boot time metrics.

42
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:58.000
Add the system boot time category to your list and apply reason code 35F9.1.

43
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:04.000
Declaring these three specific reason codes covers the foundational API footprint of the react native engine

44
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.000
and effectively satisfying Apple's automated binary scanners.

45
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.000
System APIs are only the first requirement.

46
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:16.000
You must also declare any actual user data your application collects by adding a second top level array

47
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.000
called privacy collected data types.

48
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:24.000
If your dependency tree includes external integrations like crash litix or a third party analytics SDK,

49
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.000
you add specific identifiers like crash data or device IDs to this section.

50
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:33.000
For each data type you add, answer the accompanying Boolean questions.

51
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Specify whether the data is linked to the user's identity and declare whether it is used for tracking purposes

52
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:43.000
across other applications. Once the arrays are complete, validate the configuration.

53
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:50.000
Generate a full archive build of your app so Xcode can compile the binary and check it against your new manifest.

54
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:57.000
Open the Xcode organizer, right-click your completed archive and select Generate Privacy Report.

55
00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.000
This document details every required API the compiler found.

56
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:06.000
A clean report verifies that your machine readable manifest perfectly matches the compiled binary.

57
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.000
The technical integration of your privacy manifest is now complete.

58
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:16.000
As a final validation step, upload your archived build to App Store Connect and distribute it to external testers

59
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.000
via test flight before full review.

60
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.000
External test flight distribution acts as a low-risk safety net.

61
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.000
It triggers an automated scan that checks your privacy manifest within 24 hours.

62
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:32.000
If an entry is missing, you receive immediate feedback without the penalty of a formal App Store rejection.

63
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:35.000
Treat your privacy manifest as a living document.

64
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:40.000
Whenever you add a new native dependency to your project, run a new privacy report

65
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.000
to see if that SDK introduces new API requirements.

66
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:49.000
By locking in this workflow, you eliminate the friction of automated compliance checks

67
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:54.000
and maintain a clear, predictable path for all future App Store releases.

